A parking garage charges for each stay as follows.


• Stays of 30 minutes or less are free.

• Otherwise the fee is 4 dollars for each hour or part of an hour (so 61 minutes counts as 2 hours).

• No stay costs more than 28 dollars.

```java
public class Garage {
    /** Returns the fee, in dollars, for a stay of the given length. Precondition: 0 <= minutes <= 1440 */
    public static int fee(int minutes) { /* part (a) */ }
    /** Returns the longest stay, in minutes from 0 to 1440, whose fee is at most budget. */
    public static int longestStay(int budget) { /* part (b) */ }
}
```

Examples: `fee(30)` returns 0, `fee(63)` returns 8, `fee(128)` returns 12, `fee(840)` returns 28. `longestStay(4)` returns 60.

A `ScoreKeeper` records the scores of one player. The class also tracks the total number of scores recorded by all ScoreKeeper objects.


• `ScoreKeeper(String name)` creates a keeper with no scores.

• `void addScore(int s)` records a score.

• `double getAverage()` returns the average score, or 0.0 if there are none.

• `int getBest()` returns the highest score, or -1 if there are none.

• `static int getTotalScores()` returns the number of scores recorded by all keepers.

Example:

```java
ScoreKeeper a = new ScoreKeeper("Dara");
ScoreKeeper b = new ScoreKeeper("Farah");
a.addScore(86);
a.addScore(64);
a.addScore(59);
b.addScore(52);
b.addScore(81);
a.getAverage();               // returns 69.667
a.getBest();                  // returns 86
ScoreKeeper.getTotalScores(); // returns 5
```

A `Reading` stores the day of the month and a temperature. A `SensorLog` keeps readings in increasing order of day, with no two readings on the same day.

```java
public class Reading {
    public Reading(int day, int temp) { /* not shown */ }
    public int getDay() { /* not shown */ }
    public int getTemp() { /* not shown */ }
}
public class SensorLog {
    private ArrayList<Reading> readings;
    /** Returns the number of readings with a temperature greater than t. */
    public int countAbove(int t) { /* part (a) */ }
    /** Inserts r so the list stays in increasing order of day.
        Precondition: no reading in the list has the same day as r. */
    public void addReading(Reading r) { /* part (b) */ }
}
```

If the readings are (day, temp) (7, 21), (10, 26), (11, 9), (16, 5), (24, 16), (29, 18), then `countAbove(15)` returns 4. Adding a reading for day 6 gives the days [6, 7, 10, 11, 16, 24, 29].

A `Gradebook` stores test scores in a 2D array: each row is a student and each column is a test, in the order the tests were given.

```java
public class Gradebook {
    private int[][] scores;
    /** Returns the average score on test c. */
    public double testAverage(int c) { /* part (a) */ }
    /** Returns the row index of the student whose last score minus first score is greatest.
        If there is a tie, returns the smallest such index. */
    public int mostImproved() { /* part (b) */ }
}
```

For the scores below, `testAverage(2)` returns 81.5 and `mostImproved()` returns 1.

